Replace the legal links with a source-code link.

Four places in one patch, because they are one change: everywhere the client
pointed at the upstream vendor's Terms of Service and Privacy Policy, it now
points at our source, or says nothing.

  - the About tab's "Legal" section becomes a "Licensing" section
  - the tray menu's two legal entries become one Source Code entry
  - onboarding's "By continuing, you agree to our Terms of Service and Privacy
    Policy." line is removed outright
  - the same sentence on the login window's hosting-selection page goes with it

Why the links go
----------------
Those pages belong to the upstream vendor and describe their hosted service and
their handling of your data. Neither statement is ours to make, and ZTARC has no
equivalent page yet, so brand/rules.sed would have pointed both at the marketing
site — menu entries that promise a legal document and deliver a home page. The
two consent lines are worse than that: they ask the user to agree to terms that
do not exist, and record their agreement to nothing. Removing them is the honest
option until the pages exist. Re-add them here, not in the sed rules, on the day
they do.

Why a source link takes their place
-----------------------------------
The upstream client is AGPL-3. Section 13 obliges anyone conveying a modified
version to offer its users the corresponding source, and the About tab and tray
menu are where a user would look. Deleting the only sections that carried legal
information without putting this in their place would leave the app with nowhere
to make that offer.

This is a patch rather than a sed rule because it is structural: it removes the
contents of SwiftUI Sections, an AttributedString builder and an HStack,
rewrites a section header, and no string substitution can express any of that.
scripts/audit-brand.sh asserts both halves — that the link is present, and that
the removed titles are absent — because a patch that stops applying leaves no
upstream name behind for the forbidden-word scan to catch. ztarc-windows shipped
a release exactly that way once; see its commit e13e448.

